        { "$comment": "P69 (lane cs): `__declspec(dllimport)` / `__declspec(dllexport)`. INERT here, and that is the working behaviour kept, not an omission: whether a referenced function or datum comes from another image is decided where the image is linked, from what the link holds, and what an executable exports is its object format's own statement. A function or an object; on a typedef it is ignored and warned (mingw-w64 gcc's answer; cl is silent). QUALIFIED names, and the reason is MEASURED: written `__attribute__((dllexport))` for a non-Windows target the word is warned and ignored by gcc 13.3.0 (\"attribute directive ignored\"), by clang 18.1.3 and by Apple clang (\"unknown attribute 'dllexport' ignored\") — it is no attribute there, and a plain row would make it one on every pair: silent where every reference warns, and advertised by `__has_attribute` on pairs where the word does nothing. mingw-w64 gcc takes the GNU spelling in silence on pe; here the plain spelling is reported as an unknown word on every pair, pe included (one warning, the word changes nothing either way).", "names": ["__declspec(dllimport)", "__declspec(dllexport)"], "appliesTo": ["variable", "function"], "effect": "none" },
